  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nDecent trough from a lifting upper level low combined with\ntemperature advection aloft and steep mid level lapse rates has\ncreated snow showers across the northwest this afternoon. At \ntimes these have produced moderate snowfall. This wave will \ncontinue to lift across the northwest and north central through \nthis evening gradually moving this showers northward. Perhaps \nsome light snow accumulations are possible, although main \nimpacts will be brief visibility reductions below a mile at \ntimes. Meanwhile, lingering low level moisture ahead of this \nwave will continue low clouds along with patchy fog, mist, and \nperhaps some drizzle into this evening. Dry air intrusion should\nbring some clearing to the southwest alter this afternoon into \nthis evening. Other than the southwest, most areas will see \ncooler temperatures continue through today. Tonight, some snow \nshowers may linger near the Canadian border through the late \nevening hours. Otherwise a mix of clearing to cloudy skies will \nbe found tonight. Cloudy skies look to be wedged in between \nlingering clouds in the north and increasing low level moisture \nin the south from a developing southerly flow. This southern \npush of moisture could return patchy fog and mist tonight into \nWednesday morning. Lows tonight will greatly depend on this \nclearing, and generally be in the 30s. Stacked upper low then \nstalls in southern Canada on Wednesday. Weak warm front followed\nby a weak cold front will move across the area as a result. \nThis could bring some rain showers to south central and \nsoutheastern portions, with a slight chance of snow mixed in. \nSoutherly flow ahead of the mentioned cold front could also \nbring warming temperatures in the mid 40s to mid 50s, and some \nlower 60s, provided low clouds don't linger through the day. \nCooler lows in the 20s are then forecast for Wednesday night \nbehind this mentioned front. Light winds and weak ridging could \nalso return some patchy fog Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning.\n\nRidging aloft then brings mainly dry conditions for Thursday and\nFriday, with temperatures near to perhaps slightly above normal.\nWinds will overall be light, although a breezy southerly wind\nmay develop across the west on Friday. Clusters maintain a\ngeneral ridge pattern for the upcoming weekend. As a result\nlight winds and mild temperatures are forecast. Some cluster\nsolutions try to kick out a weak wave into the plains this\nweekend, although most now keep this to our south. NBM is\nstarting to trend lower with PoPs for this weekend, although\ndoes keep in some slight chances for rain showers. Clusters\nstill showing increased rain chances early next week, yet the \nmain upper low associate with this next disturbance starting to \ntrend towards a more southern track. Depending on track of this \nsystem some breezy winds may also return early next week. \nSomething to monitor going forward.",
